The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'bigquery-public-data': {'fhir_synthea': ['patient', 'condition', 'medication_request']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their potential usage  
• patient.id: joins to both condition.subject.patientId and medication_request.subject.patientId  
• patient.name.family (inside the name array): patient’s last‑name; filter for those that begin with “A”  
• patient.deceased.boolean / patient.deceased.dateTime: identify living patients (deceased = FALSE / NULL)  
• condition.subject.patientId: joins each condition record to its patient  
• condition.code.coding.code & condition.code.text: the condition’s code / description that we must rank  
• medication_request.subject.patientId: joins each prescription to its patient  
• medication_request.status: keep only prescriptions whose status = 'active'  
• medication_request.medication.codeableConcept.coding.code: code of the prescribed medication; count DISTINCT values per patient  
• condition.id (or the whole row): allows us to COUNT DISTINCT conditions per patient  

Below are ten exploratory SQL queries—each limited to 20 rows—to move step‑by‑step from simple inspection to the full answer.  
(Replace YOUR‑PROJECT if you copy‑paste.)

```sql
-- 1. Inspect raw patient names
SELECT id,
       name
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.patient`
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Grab last names that start with “A”
SELECT p.id,
       n.family AS last_name
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.patient` AS p,
       UNNEST(p.name) AS n
WHERE  n.family LIKE 'A%'
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Living patients whose last names begin with “A”
SELECT DISTINCT p.id,
       n.family AS last_name
FROM   `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.patient` AS p,
       UNNEST(p.name) AS n
WHERE  n.family LIKE 'A%'
  AND  (p.deceased.boolean IS NULL AND p.deceased.dateTime IS NULL)
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Conditions carried by those “A*” living patients
SELECT  c.id AS condition_id,
        c.subject.patientId AS patient_id,
        c.code.coding[OFFSET(0)].code   AS cond_code,
        c.code.text                     AS cond_text
FROM    `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.condition` AS c
JOIN    (
        SELECT DISTINCT p.id
        FROM   `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.patient` AS p,
               UNNEST(p.name) AS n
        WHERE  n.family LIKE 'A%'
          AND  (p.deceased.boolean IS NULL AND p.deceased.dateTime IS NULL)
        ) AS alive_A
ON      alive_A.id = c.subject.patientId
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Count how many DISTINCT condition codes each chosen patient has
SELECT  c.subject.patientId AS patient_id,
        COUNT(DISTINCT c.code.coding[OFFSET(0)].code) AS distinct_conditions
FROM    `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.condition` AS c
JOIN    (
        SELECT DISTINCT p.id
        FROM   `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.patient` AS p,
               UNNEST(p.name) AS n
        WHERE  n.family LIKE 'A%'
          AND  (p.deceased.boolean IS NULL AND p.deceased.dateTime IS NULL)
        ) AS alive_A
ON      alive_A.id = c.subject.patientId
GROUP BY patient_id
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Only patients that have EXACTLY ONE distinct condition
SELECT  patient_id
FROM    (
        SELECT  c.subject.patientId AS patient_id,
                COUNT(DISTINCT c.code.coding[OFFSET(0)].code) AS distinct_conditions
        FROM    `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.condition` AS c
        JOIN    (
                SELECT DISTINCT p.id
                FROM   `bigquery-public-data.fhir_synthea.patient` AS p,
                       UNNEST(p.name) AS n
                WHERE  n.family LIKE 'A%'
                  AND  (p.deceased.boolean IS NULL AND p.deceased.dateTime IS NULL)
                ) AS alive_A
        ON      alive_A.id = c.subject.patientId
        GROUP  BY patient_id
        HAVING DISTINCT_CONDITIONS = 1
        )
LIMIT 20;
